  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Lateral sprain of the left ankle, grade I to II (S93.492A)</content>. Inversion injury with tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament only. She meets none of the Ottawa ankle or foot criteria for imaging: no bone tenderness at the malleoli, navicular, or fifth metatarsal, and she can bear weight for four steps. Radiographs are not indicated. [S]<list><item>Lace-up ankle brace for two to four weeks, with early protected weight bearing as pain allows; no rigid immobilization.</item><item>Cane in the right hand for the first week, especially outdoors and on the step to the studio.</item><item>Ice and elevation for the first few days, then home ankle range-of-motion and balance exercises from the handout, read aloud and given in large print.</item><item>Acetaminophen 650 mg up to every 6 hours as needed, no more than 3 g a day; topical diclofenac gel to the ankle up to four times a day is an alternative; avoid oral NSAIDs at her age.</item><item>Call for pain that is not improving by one week, numbness, or inability to bear weight, which would prompt radiographs.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Neovascular age-related macular degeneration, left eye, on treat-and-extend aflibercept (H35.3221)</content>. No new visual symptoms. The fall happened in flat, low-contrast light, when reduced contrast sensitivity from macular degeneration makes edges and steps hard to see. [S]<list><item>Keep the retina visit in about three weeks.</item><item>Walk later in the morning, when the light gives more contrast, and carry a phone; tell the housekeeper her route until the ankle has healed.</item><item>A full fall-risk review will be part of her annual wellness visit this autumn.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Age-Related Macular Degeneration Preferred Practice Pattern (2024); Diagnosis, treatment and prevention of ankle sprains: update of an evidence-based clinical guideline (Vuurberg G, et al. Br J Sports Med 2018;52(15):956) (2018)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in one week. Return sooner for worsening pain, numbness, or inability to bear weight. Annual wellness visit in October, with a fall-risk and home-safety review. Next scheduled visit 1968-10-15: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
